@node Requirements
@section Requirements

@itemize
@item
@uref{https://lean-lang.org/lean4/doc/setup.html, Lean} version 4, normally installed through @uref{https://github.com/leanprover/elan, elan}
@item
GNU Emacs version 29.1 or later
@item
The Emacs packages @uref{https://github.com/agda/agda, eri} and @uref{https://github.com/magit/magit/blob/main/lisp/magit-section.el, magit-section}, both on @uref{https://melpa.org/#/getting-started, Melpa}
@end itemize

Eglot is used for the language-server connection, and is built into
Emacs 29 and later, so nothing needs installing for it.  See
@ref{Semantic highlighting} for one feature that a newer Eglot than the
bundled one provides.

magit-section 3.3.0 is enough, and is what Debian and Ubuntu ship as
@samp{elpa-magit-section}.  If you have it, do not install a newer one on
this package's account: Magit depends on the matching version of
magit-section, so upgrading only that one breaks Magit.  Both versions
are tested, including against a live Lean server.

@node From source
@section From source

Clone the repository and add it to the @code{load-path}:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "~/path/to/lean4-mode")
@end lisp

Lean4-Mode is then enabled automatically for files with the @samp{.lean}
extension.  Loading it eagerly is only necessary in order to customize
variables before a Lean file is opened:

@lisp
(require 'lean4-mode)
@end lisp

@node With a source-based package manager
@section With a source-based package manager

List the @samp{"data"} directory in the recipe: it holds the table of
Unicode abbreviations, without which the input method has nothing to
offer.  If loading is deferred, specify @code{lean4-mode} as a @samp{:command}.

@menu
* Native @samp{vc} (Emacs 30 or later)::
* Doom-Emacs::
* Straight::
@end menu

@node Native @samp{vc} (Emacs 30 or later)
@subsection Native @samp{:vc} (Emacs 30 or later)

@lisp
(use-package lean4-mode
  :commands lean4-mode
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/d-torrance/lean4-mode.git"
       :rev :last-release
       ;; Or, for the bleeding edge:
       ;; :rev :newest
       ))
@end lisp

@node Doom-Emacs
@subsection Doom-Emacs

@lisp
(package! lean4-mode
  :recipe (:host github
           :repo "d-torrance/lean4-mode"
           :files ("*.el" "data")))
@end lisp

@node Straight
@subsection Straight

@lisp
(use-package lean4-mode
  :commands lean4-mode
  :straight (lean4-mode :type git :host github
                        :repo "d-torrance/lean4-mode"
                        :files ("*.el" "data")))
@end lisp

@node Upgrading from version 1
@section Upgrading from version 1

Version 2 replaced lsp-mode with Eglot.  If you are not ready for that,
version @samp{1.1.2} is tagged and continues to work.

@itemize
@item
lsp-mode, @samp{dash} and @samp{compat} are no longer dependencies; @samp{eri} and
Eglot are.  Any lsp-mode configuration for Lean can be removed.
@item
Diagnostics are reported through Flymake rather than Flycheck, since
that is what Eglot drives.
@item
The @code{lean4-keybinding-*} options are gone.  Keys now live in
@code{lean4-mode-map} and are rebound the ordinary way:
@samp{(keymap-set lean4-mode-map "C-c C-i" #'lean4-toggle-info)}.
@item
@code{lean4-mode-hook} is now an ordinary mode hook.  Whether the server
starts automatically is controlled by @code{lean4-auto-start-server},
which defaults to @samp{t}.
@item
@code{lean4-extra-arguments} is now @code{lean4-server-arguments}; the old name
still works.
@item
Lean 3 autodetection is gone, along with @code{lean4-autodetect-lean3}.  It
ran @samp{lean -v} every time a @samp{.lean} file was opened.  Lean 3 users
should use @uref{https://github.com/leanprover/lean3-mode, Lean3-Mode}.
@item
Four abbreviations now insert something else, the table having been
refreshed from vscode-lean4: @samp{\n} gives a literal @samp{\n} rather than ¬,
@samp{\!} gives ¬ rather than ¡, and @samp{\dot} and @samp{\cdot} give · rather than
⬝.  Seven abbreviations are gone and thirty-one are new.  This is the
change most likely to catch your fingers out; @samp{\not} is ¬ either way.
@item
Indentation cycling comes from the @samp{eri} package on Melpa, which is
the upstream of the copy this package used to vendor, so
@code{lean4-eri-indent} and @code{lean4-eri-indent-reverse} are now @code{eri-indent}
and @code{eri-indent-reverse}.  The mode no longer binds @samp{TAB} itself, so
@code{tab-always-indent} means here what it means everywhere else.
@item
Semantic highlighting needs Eglot 1.20 or later, which is newer than
the Eglot bundled with Emacs 29 or 30.  Version 1 had it
unconditionally, through lsp-mode.  See @ref{Semantic highlighting}.
@item
@code{lean4-syntax-table} is now @code{lean4-mode-syntax-table}, with the old
name kept as an obsolete alias, and the empty @code{lean4-abbrev-table} is
gone in favour of the @code{lean4-mode-abbrev-table} that
@code{define-derived-mode} makes, which is where @samp{M-x edit-abbrevs} looks.
@item
The goal display's two debounce options,
@code{lean4-info-buffer-debounce-delay-sec} and
@code{lean4-info-buffer-debounce-upper-bound-sec}, are replaced by the one
@code{lean4-info-debounce-delay}.  @code{lean4-memory-limit} and
@code{lean4-timeout-limit} are gone; neither was read by anything.
@item
@samp{lean4-debug.el} and @samp{lean4-dev.el} are gone, and with them @samp{M-x
  lean4-turn-on-debug-mode}, @samp{M-x lean4-turn-off-debug-mode}, @samp{M-x
  lean4-diff-test-file} and the @samp{*lean4-debug*} buffer.
@end itemize

@node Usage
@chapter Usage

Opening a file with the @samp{.lean} extension shows "Lean 4" in the
mode-line and starts the language server.  Files inside a Lake package
are served by @samp{lake serve}, so the package's dependencies are on the
search path; a file outside one is served by @samp{lean --server} using
whichever toolchain elan selects.

Type @samp{#check id} and Lean's answer appears in the goal display, which is
where a @samp{#check} reply belongs: Lean sends it as a note rather than as a
problem, so nothing is underlined in the file -- see @ref{Errors and warnings}.  Point on @samp{id} reports its type through ElDoc.

The @samp{*Lean Goal*} buffer, the counterpart of the Info-View pane in VS
Code, appears by itself and shows the proof state at point.  It is
displayed, not selected, so point stays in the file.  @samp{C-c C-i} hides
and shows it, and @code{lean4-info-auto-open} turns the automatic opening
off; @samp{M-x lean4-info-open} then shows it by hand, again without selecting
it.

Where it appears is up to @samp{display-buffer-alist}, as for any other
buffer.  To put it in a side window on the right:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'display-buffer-alist
             '("\\`\\*Lean Goal\\*\\'"
               (display-buffer-in-side-window)
               (side . right)
               (window-width . 0.4)))
@end lisp

