The five that act on the goal display -- @samp{C-c C-s}, @samp{C-c C-SPC},
@samp{C-c C-g}, @samp{C-c C-a} and @samp{C-c C-o} -- are bound inside the display too,
so it can be driven from either buffer.

@node Keys in the goal display
@section Keys in the goal display

Goals arrive as trees in which every subterm is labelled, so point can
rest on any part of a goal and ask about it.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Description
@tab Command
@item @samp{RET}
@tab Press the control at point, or go to a message's position
@tab @code{lean4-info-return}
@item @samp{M-.}
@tab Jump to the definition of the subterm at point
@tab @code{xref-find-definitions}
@item @samp{C-c C-t}
@tab Jump to the definition of its type
@tab @code{lean4-info-goto-type-definition}
@item @samp{TAB}
@tab Fold or unfold the trace or section at point
@tab @code{magit-section-toggle}
@item @samp{C-c C-s}
@tab Pin this position, or unpin the section point is in
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-pin}
@item @samp{C-c C-SPC}
@tab Pause or unpause the display
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-pause}
@item @samp{C-c C-g}
@tab Bring a paused display up to date
@tab @code{lean4-info-refresh-paused}
@item @samp{C-c C-a}
@tab Pause or unpause the file's message list
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-all-messages-pause}
@item @samp{C-c C-o}
@tab Order that list by position or by nearness
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-message-order}
@end multitable

Folding and movement come from @code{magit-section}, this being one of its
buffers: @samp{TAB}, @samp{n}, @samp{p}, @samp{M-n} and @samp{M-p} mean in the goal display
whatever they mean in a Magit buffer.

Pinning a position keeps it on display as a section of its own, above
the section following point, and any number of positions can be pinned.
Each keeps updating: a pin is a marker, so it follows its declaration as
the file is edited, and it stays on display after moving to another
file.  Each pinned section has its own controls -- one back to the
position, one to pause it, one to bring it up to date while paused, and
one to unpin it.  Pinning happens at point, so the followed section is
left out until point moves, rather than showing the same position twice;
returning to a pinned position later does show both.

@samp{M-x lean4-info-unpin-all} removes every pin at once, which is quicker
than unpinning several by hand; it is @samp{Unpin every position} in both
menus.

Pinning and pausing are separate, and both can hold at once: pinned
means the display has stopped following point, paused that it has
stopped updating at all.  So a pinned section still follows its
declaration through an edit until it is paused too.

Every control has a key and a menu entry as well, so none of them needs
a mouse.  The menu entries are the same in the Lean menu and in the goal
display's own, since every one of these commands works from either
buffer; each says what pressing it will do next, so an item reads
"Unpin this position" where the position is already pinned.  With
several pins there is no one pin for a key to mean, so @samp{C-c C-s} means
the section point is standing in: inside a pinned section it unpins that
one, and anywhere else it pins or unpins the position point is on in the
Lean buffer.  @samp{C-c C-SPC} and @samp{C-c C-g} read the same way: inside a
pinned section they pause and refresh that section, anywhere else the
one following point.  @samp{RET} reaches the two things in the display that
lead anywhere: on a control it does what clicking it would, and on a
message heading it goes to the place the message was reported for.

Clicking a control does what its key does, and puts the keyboard back in
the Lean buffer rather than taking focus, as VS Code does.

Where there is nothing at point to report -- outside a proof, say -- the
display says @samp{No info found.} rather than going blank, which would read
like a display that had stopped working.  Messages from elsewhere in the
file are still listed below it.

The type and docstring of the subterm at point are reported through
ElDoc, so they appear wherever ElDoc is configured to put them: the
echo area by default, or a child frame with a package such as
@samp{eldoc-box}.

Traces fold like everything else, being sections themselves: @samp{TAB} or
the mouse, with the same indicator.  Lean does not send the children of
a trace node until something asks -- a @samp{simp} trace on a real proof is
enormous -- so opening one fetches them, once.

@node The tactic state
@section The tactic state

The goals at point are listed under @samp{Tactic state}, with the number of
them on the line below, both as VS Code words and places them.  A single
goal is counted too -- @samp{1 goal} -- so the line is there on every proof
rather than only on a branching one.

A finished proof reads @samp{goals accomplished} instead, which is Lean's own
wording for it; VS Code says @samp{No goals} there and counts nothing, and
neither does this.

In the Lean buffer itself, Lean tags two things and both are marked: a
declaration whose goals are all discharged gets a @samp{✓} in the fringe
beside it, alongside Flymake's own indicators and on the same side of
the window, and a line where an "unsolved goals" error ends gets a @samp{🛠}
after it, away from the code.  @code{lean4-show-goals-accomplished} and
@code{lean4-show-unsolved-goals} turn them off; both default on, as VS Code's
do.  A terminal has no fringe to draw the check mark in and gets it in
the text; a frame whose fonts do not run to the tool gets @samp{⚒}, or
@samp{(goals)} where they run to neither.

Both take their colour from the theme in force rather than from a
colour named here: the check mark from @code{success} and the tool from
@code{warning}, through the @code{lean4-goals-accomplished} and
@code{lean4-unsolved-goals} faces.  Customize either face to overrule a
theme that colours the two alike.

The expected type at point follows, under @samp{Expected type}, and is not
counted -- VS Code counts only the tactic state.  Neither heading takes
a colon, as neither does in VS Code.

@node Copying what is on display
@section Copying what is on display

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Command
@tab Copies
@item @code{lean4-info-copy-state}
@tab the tactic state, to the kill ring
@item @code{lean4-info-copy-to-comment}
@tab the tactic state, into the Lean buffer
@item @code{lean4-info-copy-message}
@tab the message point is in, to the kill ring
@end multitable

The first two are in both menus and work from either buffer; the third
needs point to be inside a message in the goal display, messages being
sections.  @code{lean4-info-copy-to-comment} puts the state in a @samp{/- -/} block
above the line point is on, indented with it, as VS Code's "Copy Contents
to Comment" does.

The state is copied in full, whatever the settings above are hiding: text
pasted into an issue is no use with half the hypotheses missing.  This is
what VS Code copies too.

@node How much of a goal to show
@section How much of a goal to show

A goal in Mathlib can carry more hypotheses than fit on a screen, most of
them typeclass instances nobody is reasoning about.  Eight settings decide
how much of one is shown, the same eight VS Code gathers behind the cog in
its InfoView.  They are in the @samp{Lean} menu and the goal display's own,
under @samp{How much of a goal to show}, as checkboxes; each is also a command
and an option.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Setting
@tab Default
@tab Command
@item @code{lean4-info-show-goal-names}
@tab @samp{t}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-goal-names}
@item @code{lean4-info-emphasize-first-goal}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-emphasize-first-goal}
@item @code{lean4-info-target-first}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-target-first}
@item @code{lean4-info-hide-type-assumptions}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-type-assumptions}
@item @code{lean4-info-hide-instance-assumptions}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-instance-assumptions}
@item @code{lean4-info-hide-inaccessible-assumptions}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-inaccessible-assumptions}
@item @code{lean4-info-hide-let-values}
@tab @samp{nil}
@tab @code{lean4-info-toggle-let-values}
@item @code{lean4-info-expected-type-visibility}
@tab @samp{expanded}
@tab @code{lean4-info-cycle-expected-type}
@end multitable

Every default is VS Code's, so out of the box the whole goal is shown.
The commands change a setting for the session; customizing it makes the
change stick.

@itemize
@item
Goal names are the @samp{case inl} labels a structured proof produces.
@item
Emphasizing the first goal draws the others smaller and dimmer -- the
main goal is the one being worked on, the rest are context.
@item
Showing the target first puts @samp{⊢} above the hypotheses, and reverses
them, so that the target stays next to the hypothesis it was next to.
@item
Type assumptions are hypotheses that are themselves types, such as the
implicit @samp{α : Type} of a general lemma.  Instance assumptions are
typeclass instances, @samp{[Inhabited α]} and its kind.  Hiding either drops
the hypothesis entirely.
@item
Inaccessible assumptions are the ones Lean prints with a dagger, @samp{h✝},
which cannot be referred to by name.  They are dimmed rather than
hidden by default.  Where a dagger name shares a line with a usable
one, hiding takes away just the name.
@item
Hiding let-values shows a @samp{let} binder's type but not its value, which
on a large value is the difference between reading the goal and
scrolling past it.
@item
The expected type can be shown, shown folded, or left out.  The middle
two are defaults rather than rules: the section still folds and unfolds
with @samp{TAB}, and a fold made by hand survives until the setting changes.
@end itemize

Goals are kept as Lean sent them and rendered on the way into the
buffer, so changing one of these costs a redraw rather than another
request.  That is what lets a change reach a pinned or paused section,
where re-fetching would be precisely the wrong thing.  Goals from a
server too old for the interactive RPC arrive as text and cannot be
filtered; the settings simply have nothing to act on.

@node Errors and warnings
@section Errors and warnings

Diagnostics are reported through Flymake, which Eglot drives.  @samp{C-c ! n}
and @samp{C-c ! p} move between them, and @code{flymake-show-buffer-diagnostics}
lists them.

@samp{M-x lean4-diagnostics-next-sorry} moves to the next declaration still
admitted with @samp{sorry}, skipping every other kind of diagnostic; a prefix
argument moves that many.  It is built on Flymake rather than beside it,
and it wraps round to the first when there are none left below point.
VS Code leaves this to its Problems view.

Not everything Lean sends is a problem.  It reports a completed proof,
trace output and whatever a project's own tactics emit at note severity,
all of which is meant for the goal display; flagging it in the editor
buries the errors among it.  So Flymake is shown errors and warnings,
and the rest goes to the goal display only.  Set
@code{lean4-flymake-minimum-severity} to @samp{note} to be shown everything, as VS
Code's Problems view does, or to @samp{error} for less.

Messages appear in the goal display placed by Lean's own notion of
which declaration they belong to, so a message about a multi-line
declaration stays visible while point moves through it.  Terms inside a
message are as interactive as terms inside a goal.

Those covering point are listed under @samp{Messages}, set in under the
heading naming the position, along with the goals and the expected type.
Every message in the file is listed under @samp{All messages}, at the outer
level, with two controls of its own: one orders the list by nearness to
point or by position in the file -- nearness first by default, as in VS
Code, whose manual has All Messages "sorted by their proximity to the
text cursor by default" -- and one holds the list as it is, separately
from the pause in the position heading.  While the display is paused a
third control appears in the position heading, between the way back to
the pinned position and the pin, which brings it up to date without
unpausing it.  Both headings count what is in them by severity, the way
VS Code's do -- @samp{All messages (1 ⊗  5 ⓘ)} for a file with one error and
five notes.

Messages Lean marks as being for the goal display rather than the
editor -- that is what @samp{isSilent} means, the completed-proof report
among them -- are shown against the position they belong to and kept out
of both Flymake and @samp{All messages}, as VS Code does with them.

Lean emits three severities: error @samp{⊗}, warning @samp{⚠} and information
@samp{ⓘ}.  LSP defines a fourth, hint, which this package draws as @samp{ⓗ}, but
Lean's own @code{MessageSeverity} has no such value and its server never
sends one.  The glyphs are picked for the frame, falling back to @samp{E W I
H} where none can be displayed.

Each message is a section of its own, so a long one -- a @samp{simp} trace, a
mismatch between two large terms -- folds away without folding the rest.
Sections are set in with spaces and their fold indicators drawn by
@code{magit-section}, in the fringe, the way a Magit buffer does both.
Its heading names the file, line and column, and carries an @samp{↪} control,
right-aligned, that sends point there.  Clicking works as @samp{mouse-1} does
throughout Emacs: on a control it acts, and anywhere else it moves
point -- which is how a subterm is put under ElDoc, and how VS Code
reads a plain click too.  @samp{RET} does the same two things by keyboard,
going to the message's position from anywhere on its heading; the
definition of a subterm is @samp{M-.}, wherever your xref frontend puts it.
Folding is @samp{TAB}, and by mouse whatever
@code{magit-section} itself offers -- a double click, or a click on the
fringe or margin, from version 4; nothing in 3.3.0 -- so this display
folds the way your Magit does.  The same control appears in the
top heading, but only while pinned: unpinned, the display is following
point and it would only lead where point already is.

@node Building dependencies
@section Building dependencies

Visiting a file does not build what it imports.  The server decides that
from a field on the notification announcing the file, and @samp{C-c C-d}
(@code{lean4-refresh-file-dependencies}) is what asks for a build: it closes
and reopens the file, telling Lean to build a dependency that is out of
date on the way.  That is the command to reach for after editing an
import, or when the goal display reports an import that cannot be found.
VS Code calls it "Server: Restart File".

The build happens once.  If the file worker then crashes, reopening the
file does not set the build going again -- a distinction worth having on a
file whose imports do not compile, where the alternative is a rebuild per
crash.

Set @code{lean4-automatically-build-dependencies} to @samp{t} to build on every
open instead, as VS Code's @code{lean4.automaticallyBuildDependencies} does.
Both default off for the same reason: on a project the size of Mathlib,
opening one file to read it should not commit you to building everything
beneath it.  @samp{C-c C-p C-l} (@code{lean4-lake-build}) builds the package
outright.

@node Starting a project
@section Starting a project

Three commands, in the @samp{Lean} menu under @samp{Project}.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Command
@tab Creates
@item @code{lean4-new-project}
@tab a new project on the current stable Lean
@item @code{lean4-new-mathlib-project}
@tab a new project depending on Mathlib
@item @code{lean4-download-project}
@tab a clone of an existing project
@end multitable

Each asks for a directory, which has to be empty or absent -- @samp{lake init}
expects to be what fills it -- and for a new project its last component
becomes the package name, so it wants to be something Lean will accept as
a module name.  Dired opens on the project once it has been built.

@code{lean4-new-project} runs @samp{lake init}, then @samp{lake update}, then @samp{lake
build}, then commits the result; @code{lean4-new-mathlib-project} adds @samp{lake
init}'s @samp{math} template and fetches Mathlib's cache before building,
without which the first build compiles Mathlib from source.  These are the
steps VS Code's "Create Standalone Project" and "Create Project Using
Mathlib" take.

@code{lean4-download-project} offers Mathlib and @uref{https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathematics_in_lean, Mathematics in Lean} by name, as
VS Code does, and takes any Git URL besides.  After cloning it does what
@samp{C-c C-p C-l} does: resolve dependencies, fetch the cache, build.

The initial commit uses your own Git identity where one is configured, and
falls back to a placeholder where none is, so that a machine without
@samp{user.name} set does not fail the whole creation on its last step.  VS Code
commits as itself for the same reason.

@node Building
@section Building

@samp{C-c C-p C-l} (@code{lean4-lake-build}) builds the package the way VS Code's
"Build Project" does, which is three steps rather than a bare @samp{lake
build}:

@enumerate
@item
@samp{lake resolve-deps} clones a dependency the manifest names but the
checkout has not got.
@item
@samp{lake exe cache get} downloads Mathlib's build artefacts, where that
executable exists -- see @ref{Mathlib's build cache}.  On a project that
depends on Mathlib this is the step between minutes and hours.
@item
@samp{lake build} builds what is left.
@end enumerate

Each step is conditional on the one before, so a failure stops the rest,
and all three report into one compilation buffer where @samp{M-x next-error}
walks whatever any of them said.

The cache step is skipped, without complaint, in a project that has no
@samp{cache} executable -- most projects.  A cache download that @emph{fails},
though, stops the build rather than leaving it to do that work locally.

For a bare @samp{lake build}, with no network and no dependency resolution,
@samp{M-x compile} is the thing.

@node Mathlib's build cache
@section Mathlib's build cache

Mathlib is far too large to build locally, and does not need to be: its
artifacts are built upstream and can be downloaded.  Three commands ask
for them, all through @samp{lake exe cache get}, which is Mathlib's own
executable rather than anything Lake provides -- so in a project that
does not depend on Mathlib they fail, and Lake says so in the
compilation buffer.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Description
@tab Command
@item @samp{C-c C-p C-c}
@tab The whole package
@tab @code{lean4-lake-fetch-cache}
@item @samp{C-c C-p C-f}
@tab This file and what it imports
@tab @code{lean4-lake-fetch-file-cache}
@item 
@tab Every open Lean file in the package
@tab @code{lean4-lake-fetch-open-file-caches}
@end multitable

The last is in the Lean menu, under @samp{Project}, along with the other two
and @samp{lake build}.  Fetching for one file is much quicker than for the
whole of Mathlib and is usually what is wanted; fetching for the package
is the thing to run once, after cloning.

Output goes to a compilation buffer, so @samp{M-x next-error} walks whatever
Lake reports, as with any other build.

@node Choosing which Lean to use
@section Choosing which Lean to use

Four commands, in the @samp{Lean} menu under @samp{Lean version}.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Command
@tab Sets
@item @code{lean4-select-project-toolchain}
@tab this project's @samp{lean-toolchain}
@item @code{lean4-select-default-toolchain}
@tab the version used outside any project
@item @code{lean4-update-release-channel}
@tab fetches a channel's newest version
@item @code{lean4-uninstall-toolchains}
@tab removes installed versions
@end multitable

All four need elan 4 or later, which is where @samp{elan dump-state} arrives;
VS Code requires the same, for the same reason -- without it the versions
cannot be listed with what elan has resolved them to.

The versions offered are not only the ones installed.  As VS Code does,
@samp{release.lean-lang.org} is asked for the stable, beta and nightly
releases too, so a version you have not got can be chosen and elan
fetches it; each is marked @samp{(installed)} or @samp{(not installed)}.  Being
unable to reach that list is a warning, not a failure: what is installed
is still worth offering.  Releases are listed newest first, with a release
above the candidates that led to it.  @code{lean4-toolchain-releases-url} is
the address asked, for a mirror or an air-gapped copy of it.

Only @code{lean4-select-default-toolchain} offers @samp{leanprover/lean4:stable},
the channel meaning "whatever is newest".  A project pins a version
instead, which is the same distinction VS Code draws.

@code{lean4-update-release-channel} offers only the channels actually behind,
showing what each is installed at and what it would become, and says so
where none are.  @code{lean4-uninstall-toolchains} marks each version as unused
or names the projects still using it, from @samp{elan toolchain gc}; it takes
several at once, comma-separated.

Each asks before acting.  Setting a project's version asks hardest, and
the warning is VS Code's: it is meant for maintainers of a project, and
changing it can break not only the project's own code but its lakefile and
its Lake dependencies.  To take a dependency's version instead, in order
to stay compatible with it, @code{lean4-lake-update-dependency} is the command
to use.

@menu
* Elan itself::
* Git and curl::
@end menu

@node Elan itself
@subsection Elan itself

Three more commands manage the tool rather than the versions.

@code{lean4-install-elan} fetches and runs the installer from
@samp{elan.lean-lang.org}, which is what VS Code does and what Lean's @uref{https://lean-lang.org/lean4/doc/setup.html, setup
guide} describes.  It shows the exact command and asks before running it,
since this downloads a script and executes it; output goes to a
compilation buffer.  It refuses where elan is already installed.  Emacs
will not find the new elan until the environment it was started with is
refreshed, which usually means restarting Emacs.

@code{lean4-update-elan} runs @samp{elan self update}.

@code{lean4-uninstall-elan} runs @samp{elan self uninstall}, which takes every
installed Lean version with it -- so every project on the machine loses
the Lean it was using, and Lean and Lake leave the @samp{PATH}.  It asks
twice.

@code{lean4-install-toolchain} downloads a version without making it the
default, for fetching one ahead of needing it; choosing a @samp{(not
installed)} version in any of the other commands fetches it anyway.

@node Git and curl
@subsection Git and curl

Elan downloads toolchains with curl and Lake clones dependencies with Git,
so neither works without them.  @code{lean4-install-dependencies} checks for
both -- the same two VS Code checks -- and offers either to run the
install command or to put it in the kill ring, which is the choice VS Code
offers as @samp{Proceed} and @samp{Copy Script to Clipboard}.  The command is shown
either way.  Where nothing is missing, it says so and stops.

What it offers depends on the system:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem System
@tab Runs
@tab Copies
@item Debian and the like
@tab @samp{pkexec ... apt-get install}
@tab @samp{sudo apt install}
@item Fedora and the like
@tab @samp{pkexec dnf install}
@tab @samp{sudo dnf install}
@item macOS
@tab Apple's Command Line Tools
@tab the same script
@item Windows with winget
@tab @samp{winget install Git.Git}
@tab the same command
@end multitable

On Linux it is @samp{pkexec} that asks for the password, and it is the @samp{sudo}
form that gets copied.  Without @samp{pkexec} there is nothing to run and only
the @samp{sudo} command is offered.  On Windows without winget, and on any
system whose package manager is not recognised, this says what to install
and stops.

Unlike VS Code, no pinned Git installer is downloaded on Windows without
winget: a version pinned in this package would go stale between releases,
so it points at @uref{https://git-scm.com/download/win, git-scm.com} instead, which is always current.

As with elan, Emacs will not find a newly installed program until the
environment it was started with is refreshed.

@node Searching Mathlib with Loogle
@section Searching Mathlib with Loogle

@samp{M-x lean4-loogle-search} asks @uref{https://loogle.lean-lang.org, Loogle}, which searches Mathlib by shape
rather than by name:

@itemize
@item
@samp{Nat.succ_le_succ} finds the declaration of that name.
@item
@samp{?a + ?b = ?b + ?a} finds the commutativity lemmas.
@item
@samp{Real.sin, Real.cos} finds what mentions both.
@item
A query beginning with a turnstile, @samp{|-} followed by a pattern, finds
what @emph{concludes} a statement of that shape.
@end itemize

Loogle's own page documents the syntax.

The query is read with the Lean input method inherited, so @samp{\alpha} becomes
α in the minibuffer exactly as it would in a Lean buffer -- which is the
point of asking from inside Emacs rather than in a browser tab, since
Loogle's syntax leans on notation.  An active region is offered as the
starting point.

Results appear in a @samp{*Lean Loogle*} buffer: each is its name, the module it
lives in, and its type, foldable like everything else.  Where Loogle offers
a spelling suggestion it is a button that searches for it instead.

@multitable {aaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Description
@tab Command
@item @samp{RET}
@tab Open the result's documentation in a browser
@tab @code{lean4-loogle-browse-documentation}
@item @samp{w}
@tab Copy the result's name
@tab @code{lean4-loogle-copy-name}
@item @samp{g}
@tab Ask Loogle the same query again
@tab @code{lean4-loogle-again}
@item @samp{q}
@tab Bury the buffer
@tab @code{quit-window}
@end multitable

@samp{RET} does what clicking a result's name does in VS Code.

This needs no Lean server and no project: Loogle is a web service, and it
answers about Mathlib rather than about the code in hand.
@code{lean4-loogle-url} points elsewhere, for a Loogle of your own, and
@code{lean4-loogle-documentation-url} says where @samp{RET} should look a result up
-- by default @samp{leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/}, to which
the module's name, its dots turned into slashes, and @samp{.html} are
appended.

@node The module hierarchy
@section The module hierarchy

What a module imports, and what imports it, as a tree in a
@samp{*Lean Module Hierarchy*} buffer.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Command
@tab Shows
@item @code{lean4-module-hierarchy}
@tab what this file's module imports
@item @code{lean4-module-hierarchy-inverse}
@tab what imports this file's module
@item @code{lean4-module-copy-name}
@tab copies the module's name
@end multitable

All three are in the @samp{Lean} menu under @samp{Module hierarchy}.  VS Code binds
its two to @samp{Alt+Shift+M} and @samp{Alt+Shift+N}; no keys are bound here, those
chords not being Emacs ones, so bind them to taste:

@lisp
(keymap-set lean4-mode-map "C-c C-p C-m" #'lean4-module-hierarchy)
@end lisp

The buffer is a @code{magit-section} tree, so it folds with @samp{TAB} and moves the
way the goal display does.

@multitable {aaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Description
@tab Command
@item @samp{RET}
@tab Visit the file of the module at point
@tab @code{lean4-module-visit}
@item @samp{TAB}
@tab Fold or unfold the node at point
@tab @code{magit-section-toggle}
@item @samp{g}
@tab Ask for the tree again
@tab @code{lean4-module-refresh}
@item @samp{q}
@tab Bury the buffer
@tab @code{quit-window}
@end multitable

A level is fetched when it is opened, not before: Mathlib's import graph
is far too large to ask for at once.  An unopened node shows @samp{...} until
its children arrive.  A module that already appears further up the tree is
marked @samp{(shown above)} and not expanded, since the import graph has
diamonds and following one would go round for ever.

Imports of a module using Lean's @samp{module} keyword carry modifiers, and
those are shown beside the name: @samp{private}, @samp{all}, @samp{meta}, or @samp{meta and
non-meta} for one imported both ways.

@menu
* It needs a built project::
@end menu

@node It needs a built project
@subsection It needs a built project

The server answers these from its reference data, which it builds from the
@samp{.ilean} files a build leaves behind.  In a project that has not been
built the tree is empty -- correctly so, there being nothing indexed to
report.  Run @samp{C-c C-p C-l} (@code{lean4-lake-build}), or
@samp{C-c C-p C-c} (@code{lean4-lake-fetch-cache}) on a Mathlib project, first.

@code{lean4-enable-file-watchers} is off by default, so a tree fetched after a
rebuild may be the one from before it; @samp{g} asks again.

@node Other Lake operations
@section Other Lake operations

Two more commands live in the same @samp{Project} menu.

@code{lean4-lake-clean} runs @samp{lake clean}, and asks first: on a project the
size of Mathlib that discards hours of building, though
@code{lean4-lake-fetch-cache} gets most of it back.

@code{lean4-lake-update-dependency} runs @samp{lake update} on one dependency,
offering the package's direct Git dependencies for completion -- read
from @samp{lake-manifest.json}, which is Lake's record of what it resolved
them to.  It asks first too, and means it: VS Code's warning is that the
command is for maintainers of a project, and that if the new version is
incompatible with another dependency or with the code here, the project
may stop building.  It also rewrites the manifest, which most projects
keep under version control.

Unlike VS Code it leaves @samp{lean-toolchain} alone.  VS Code offers to match
it to the updated dependency's; that is a second decision, about a file
both Lake and elan read, and is better made deliberately.

Opening a project needs nothing from this package: a Lean file is served
by whichever workspace encloses it, so @samp{C-x C-f} and @samp{project.el} are the
whole of it.  VS Code's "Open Local Project" exists because it has a
window to point at a folder.

@node Moving by declaration
@section Moving by declaration

The commands Emacs binds in every buffer for moving over a function work
here over a declaration, so @samp{C-M-a} goes back to the start of the
@samp{theorem} or @samp{def} point is in, @samp{C-M-e} forward to its end, and
@samp{C-M-h} (@code{mark-defun}) and @samp{M-x narrow-to-defun} take the whole of it.
A prefix argument repeats, and a negative one reverses.

A declaration for this purpose is a named one -- @samp{theorem}, @samp{lemma},
@samp{def}, @samp{abbrev}, @samp{instance}, @samp{example}, @samp{structure}, @samp{class},
@samp{inductive}, @samp{opaque}, @samp{axiom}, @samp{macro}, @samp{syntax}, @samp{elab}, @samp{notation}
and their relatives.  @samp{namespace} and @samp{section} are not: they enclose
declarations rather than being them, and a @samp{C-M-a} that stopped at
@samp{namespace Foo} would leave the theorem you were standing in.

It begins at whatever is attached above it -- its attributes, and the
doc comment those hang from -- so @code{mark-defun} does not leave the
docstring behind.  A blank line detaches, a @samp{/- -/} with a gap under it
being a note about the file rather than about what follows; a @samp{--}
comment never attaches, having no way to say whether it belongs to the
line below or the one above.  The end is where the next declaration
begins, with the blank lines between the two belonging to neither.

@code{which-function-mode} and @samp{M-x add-change-log-entry} are told the name
of the declaration point is in, which is what makes them work here as
they do elsewhere.  An @samp{example}, or an @samp{instance} not given a name,
answers with its keyword instead.

None of this asks Lean.  The server could answer it exactly, through
the document symbols it already sends -- which is where VS Code's
outline, breadcrumbs bar and go-to-symbol come from, and which reach
Emacs as Imenu through Eglot.  But moving by structure has to work in a
file that has not been elaborated, or has no server, or does not
compile, which is precisely when one moves around by structure.  So
this reads the buffer, as @code{lean4-indent-line} does.

Declarations inside a @samp{mutual} block count one by one.  Lean writes
them flush with the @samp{mutual}, so nothing short of counting @samp{mutual} and
@samp{end} would tell them from top-level ones, and reaching one of them is
worth more than reporting the block as a unit.

@node Applying what Lean suggests
@section Applying what Lean suggests

Lean offers to write things for you in two quite different ways, and
@samp{C-c C-.} (@code{lean4-apply-suggestion}) asks for both and offers them in one
list.  VS Code binds @samp{Ctrl+.} to the first group, which is where the
mnemonic comes from.

Most suggestions arrive as LSP @emph{code actions}:

@itemize
@item
the @samp{Try this} of @samp{simp?}, @samp{exact?}, @samp{apply?} and @samp{rw?}, which is how
those tactics report what they found -- @samp{"Try this: simp only"} and the
lemmas it used;
@item
an import, or a spelling, offered for an unknown identifier;
@item
the output @samp{#guard_msgs} wants in place of the docstring above it;
@item
whatever a project's own code actions provide -- Batteries offers
several, including an instance skeleton and the cases of an
@samp{induction}.
@end itemize

One arrives as an @emph{inlay hint} instead: see @ref{What Lean filled in for you}.
Both read the same way -- Lean worked something out and is offering to
put it in the file -- so both are on the one key, the code actions first
and the hint last.

Point decides what is asked about: an active region, failing that any
diagnostic covering point, so that a fix offered for an error is found
from anywhere within it, failing that the term at point, and failing
that point itself.  Where Lean offers nothing it says so and stops.

Carrying out a code action is handed back to Eglot, which resolves it,
applies its edit and runs its command; @samp{M-x eglot-code-actions} is the
same thing without the inlay hint.

Most terminals cannot send @samp{C-.} at all -- only those speaking one of
the newer keyboard protocols can -- so in one of the others, rebind it:

@lisp
(keymap-set lean4-mode-map "C-c C-y" #'lean4-apply-suggestion)
@end lisp

Nothing else needs changing: what the goal display and ElDoc say about
the key follows the keymap.

@node What Lean filled in for you
@section What Lean filled in for you

A declaration whose type mentions a variable never bound still compiles:

@example
def myId (a : α) : α := a
@end example

Lean binds @samp{α} itself and reports having done so as an @emph{inlay hint}, a
greyed-out = @{α@}= drawn after the name without being in the file.  Eglot
draws it.  Two things the server attaches to it are read here.

With point on that line, ElDoc says what @samp{α} was inferred to be, and
names the key that writes the binder down:

@example
Automatically-inserted implicit parameters:
α : Sort u_1
C-c C-. offers to insert " @{α@}"
@end example


@samp{C-c C-.} (@code{lean4-apply-suggestion}) then offers it, along with anything
else Lean suggests here -- see @ref{Applying what Lean suggests}.  Taking it
makes the binder real, leaving point where it was:

@example
def myId @{α@} (a : α) : α := a
@end example

The text and its position are Lean's own rather than a guess, so the
binders land in the order and place Lean chose.

This is VS Code's hover on the hint, which shows the types, and its
double-click, which inserts them.  Emacs has neither gesture, so the
report goes through ElDoc -- where the type of the subterm under point
already goes, and which Eglot has set to compose, so this is shown
alongside the hover rather than instead of it -- and the insertion is a
command.  Announcing a command in the report is how Eglot itself offers
a code action, there being no lightbulb to click; the announcement is
written with @code{substitute-command-keys}, so it names whichever key you
have bound and the command itself where none is.

@samp{M-x lean4-insert-inlay-hint} is the hint on its own, without the list,
for a key of your own that means only this.

Set @code{lean4-inlay-hints-in-eldoc} to @samp{nil} to stop the report, and with it
the request ElDoc would otherwise make each time point comes to rest;
the command still works.  @code{lean4-inlay-hint-timeout} is how long that
command waits for an answer.

Auto-bound implicits are the only hint Lean sends as of 4.33, and
Mathlib sets @samp{autoImplicit} to false, so in a Mathlib file there are no
hints and nothing to report.  Nothing here is particular to that hint,
though: whatever the server sends is what is shown and inserted.

@node Completion
@section Completion

Completion is offered through @code{completion-at-point-functions}, so any
completion interface works: the default @samp{*Completions*} buffer,
@samp{company}, @samp{corfu} or anything else.  Three sources are active in a Lean
buffer.  Eglot's is one.  Another is for the Unicode abbreviations, so
that typing @samp{\al} offers @samp{\alpha} and inserting it produces α.  The
third is for Lean's keywords, because the server's own completion is
contextual and offers nothing towards a bare word at the top level:
@samp{exam} gets no help towards @samp{example} from Lean, and VS Code fills the
same gap with editor-level word suggestions.

None of the three is exclusive, Eglot's included -- its entry is
rewrapped to say so.  Left as Eglot leaves it, a prefix the server had
nothing for ended completion there instead of falling through to
whatever else you have configured.

Typing an abbreviation in full still works without completion: the Lean
input method replaces it as soon as it is unambiguous.

@node Finding a symbol you cannot name
@section Finding a symbol you cannot name

Completion goes from an abbreviation to a symbol, which is no help when
the abbreviation is what you do not know.  Three commands go the other
way, listing every symbol with the abbreviations that produce it -- so a
symbol can be found by typing either it or one of them.  They are in the
@samp{Lean} menu under @samp{Unicode input}, and are VS Code's three.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Command
@tab Does
@item @code{lean4-input-insert-symbol}
@tab inserts the symbol at point
@item @code{lean4-input-copy-symbol}
@tab puts it in the kill ring
@item @code{lean4-input-find-symbol}
@tab reports its abbreviations, then offers both
@end multitable

The shortest abbreviation comes first, that being the one worth learning.
Copying is for pasting somewhere the input method does not reach -- another
program, or a prompt with no input method of its own.

@samp{C-c C-k} goes the other way for a symbol already in the buffer: it says
which keystrokes would have produced the one at point.  @samp{M-x
lean4-input-show-translations} lists the whole table.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

Lean4-Mode uses @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/eglot/, Eglot}, the LSP client built into Emacs 29 and later, so
Eglot's own configuration applies.

@menu
* Frequently used options::
* Finding the Lean programs::
* Tuning::
* The goal display's controls::
* Older Lean versions::
* Semantic highlighting::
* Optional packages::
@end menu

@node Frequently used options
@section Frequently used options

@itemize
@item
@code{lean4-auto-start-server} (default @samp{t}) -- whether visiting a Lean
file starts the language server.  Set to @samp{nil} to start it by hand
with @samp{M-x eglot}.
@item
@code{lean4-info-auto-open} (default @samp{t}) -- whether the goal display
appears when a Lean file is opened, as VS Code's InfoView does.
@item
@code{lean4-info-interactive} (default @samp{t}) -- whether to fetch goals over
Lean's interactive RPC@.  With @samp{nil}, goals are plain text and cannot
be hovered or jumped from.
@item
@code{lean4-indent-offset} (default @samp{2}) -- columns per level of
indentation.
@item
@code{lean4-indent-function} -- what a @samp{TAB} you pressed runs.
@code{lean4-indent-line} guesses from the layout and cycles when pressed
again; @code{eri-indent} does not guess and only cycles, which is what this
mode did before version 2.  Only a @samp{TAB} consults it: @code{indent-region}
and @code{newline-and-indent} always take the guess, once, so that
reindenting a region gives the same answer however often it is done.
Since the mode binds no @samp{TAB} of its own, @code{tab-always-indent} means
here what it means everywhere else.
@item
@code{lean4-enable-file-watchers} (default @samp{nil}) -- whether to honour the
server's request to watch every @samp{*.ilean} file.  On a project the size
of Mathlib that is tens of thousands of files.
@item
@code{lean4-automatically-build-dependencies} (default @samp{nil}) -- whether
visiting a file may build the dependencies it imports.  See @ref{Building dependencies}.
@item
@code{lean4-server-arguments} -- extra arguments for the language server.
@item
@code{lean4-workspace-roots} and @code{lean4-workspace-exclusions} -- directories
to treat as workspace roots, and directories in which never to start a
server.
@end itemize

@node Finding the Lean programs
@section Finding the Lean programs

Normally nothing here needs setting: elan puts @samp{lean}, @samp{lake} and @samp{elan}
on your @samp{PATH}, and elan's shims select whichever toolchain the project
in hand pins.

@itemize
@item
@code{lean4-rootdir} (default @samp{nil}) -- a directory holding the toolchain,
for a Lean that is not on @code{exec-path} at all.  Leave it nil under
elan: naming a directory here reaches past the shims, and so past the
per-project version they exist to choose.
@item
@code{lean4-executable-name}, @code{lean4-lake-name} and @code{lean4-elan-name}
(@samp{lean}, @samp{lake} and @samp{elan}, the first two with @samp{.exe} on Windows) --
what each of the three programs is called, for an installation that
renames one or puts a wrapper in front of it.
@end itemize

@node Tuning
@section Tuning

@itemize
@item
@code{lean4-diagnostics-delay} (default @samp{0.05} seconds) -- how long to let
diagnostics gather before reacting to them.  Lean republishes them
repeatedly while a file elaborates and the goal display is rebuilt
from each batch, so this is the difference between reading the display
and watching it flicker.  Raise it on a slow machine.
@item
@code{lean4-info-debounce-delay} (default @samp{0.1} seconds) -- how much quiet
to wait for before re-rendering the goal display.  Point moves in
bursts and @code{magit-section} rendering is not cheap, so rapid movement
is coalesced into one redraw at the end of it.
@end itemize

@node The goal display's controls
@section The goal display's controls

Each control says what clicking it will do rather than what the state
is, so both swap glyph when engaged: @samp{📌} becomes @samp{📍} once pinned, and
@samp{⏸} becomes @samp{⏵} once paused.  The pin is the same pin seen head-on
instead of from the side -- pushed in rather than held ready.  VS Code
turns its pin from upright to flat to say the same thing, but that is a
codicon font; Unicode's one upright/slanted pin pair needs U+1F588,
which too many fonts draw as a box to rest the difference on.  The
engaged control is faced differently too, which is what carries the
state on a frame with no second glyph to swap to.

The glyphs are chosen for whichever frame they are drawn in, decided
each time rather than once at startup, so one Emacs serving both a
graphical frame and a terminal gets the right thing in each.  A
graphical frame usually gets @samp{📌 📍 ⏸ ⏵}, a terminal @samp{⌖ ⌾ ⏸ ⏵}, and a
display with nothing but ASCII gets @samp{P [P] || >}.

Every one of the seven is an option, so any of them can be set to a
string of your own instead: @code{lean4-info-pin-icon},
@code{lean4-info-unpin-icon}, @code{lean4-info-pause-icon} and
@code{lean4-info-resume-icon} for those four, and @code{lean4-info-goto-icon}
(@samp{↪}), @code{lean4-info-refresh-icon} (@samp{⟳}) and @code{lean4-info-sort-icon} (@samp{⇅})
for the way to a position, the refresh of a paused section and the order
of the message list.  The last three do not swap glyph: going somewhere
and refreshing have no second state, and neither message order is the
one to warn about, so the face says which is in force and the tooltip
says what a click would change it to.

@node Older Lean versions
@section Older Lean versions

Some of what this package reads was added to the Lean server over time,
and it degrades quietly where a field is absent.

Lean marks messages meant for the goal display rather than the editor,
and marks a finished declaration with a tag.  Servers older than those
fields -- 4.26 sets neither, even when asked -- send the completed-proof
report as an ordinary note, so it is the severity filter above rather
than the tag that keeps it out of the editor there.  The marker in the
goal display needs the tag, and is simply absent without it.

Interactive references are encoded in a format negotiated with the
server; version 1 arrived in Lean 4.30.  Older servers keep to version
0, which this package reads too.  @samp{M-x lean4-diagnose} reports which
extensions the running server offers.

@node Semantic highlighting
@section Semantic highlighting

What counts as a keyword in Lean is settled by the elaborator rather
than by the syntax, so the server is the only thing that can say.  It
reports its findings as LSP semantic tokens, and gives @samp{sorry} and the
other proof-admitting syntax a token type of its own,
@samp{leanSorryLike}.  Eglot gained support for these in version 1.20, which
is newer than the Eglot bundled with Emacs 29 (1.12) or Emacs 30
(1.17).  Lean4-Mode uses it when it is available and says nothing when
it is not, so

@example
M-x package-install RET eglot RET
@end example


turns on semantic highlighting on any supported Emacs.

Version 1 had it unconditionally, lsp-mode having supported semantic
tokens throughout, so this is the one thing the move to Eglot can take
away from a version 1 user who installs nothing.

Expect keywords rather than colour for every identifier.  The server
advertises the whole standard set of token types but, as of Lean 4.32,
sends little beyond keywords and @samp{leanSorryLike}; put point on the
@samp{rfl} in

@example
theorem proved : 1 + 1 = 2 := by
  rfl
@end example

and @samp{M-:} @samp{(get-text-property (point) 'face)} answers
@samp{eglot-semantic-keyword}.  Nothing here could have known that: @samp{rfl} is
a keyword because the elaborator says so, and @samp{M-x
eglot-semantic-tokens-mode} to turn it off leaves it uncoloured.

@node Optional packages
@section Optional packages

None of these are required, and none are declared as dependencies.

@itemize
@item
@samp{markdown-mode} -- renders docstrings in hover popups.
@item
@samp{eldoc-box} -- shows ElDoc output in a child frame rather than the
echo area.
@item
@samp{company} or @samp{corfu} -- a completion interface.
@item
@samp{consult-eglot} -- searching workspace symbols.
@end itemize

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@samp{M-x lean4-diagnose} reports how things are set up: the versions of
Emacs and of each dependency, the Lean and Lake executables it found,
the workspace root and its toolchain, the command used to start the
server and whether one is running, and the diagnostics currently in the
buffer with Lean's own fields on each.  It reports on a Lean buffer even
when run from somewhere else.

Include its output when reporting a problem; the counterpart in VS Code
is "Troubleshooting: Show Setup Information".

@samp{M-x lean4-show-version} reports just the Lean version, when that is all
that is wanted.

@samp{C-c C-x} or @samp{C-c C-l} (@code{lean4-std-exe}) runs Lean over the file on its
own, outside the server, which is the quickest way to see what the
compiler alone makes of it.  @samp{M-x lean4-execute} does the same but asks
first for arguments to pass to @samp{lean}.  Both run inside @samp{lake env} where
the file belongs to a package, so that its dependencies are found, and
both go through @samp{compile}, so @samp{M-x next-error} walks whatever Lean
reports.

Note that it is the file on disk that is compiled, not the buffer: save
first, or what Lean reports will be about the previous version.  A buffer
visiting no file at all is written to a temporary one instead.

@menu
* Where the documentation is::
* "Timed out after 30 seconds" opening a file outside a project::
* "Imports are out of date and must be rebuilt"::
@end menu

