All four are in the @samp{Lean} menu under @samp{Help}, along with @samp{M-x
lean4-diagnose}.  The first two are VS Code's "Show Manual" and "Show
Troubleshooting Guide", pointed at this package's manual rather than the
extension's; the other two open the same pages VS Code opens.

The manual is shipped as Info beside the source, so it is found whether or
not the package was installed anywhere Info knows to look.  The same text
is @uref{https://d-torrance.github.io/lean4-mode/, on the web}, rebuilt from
this file whenever it changes.

@node "Timed out after 30 seconds" opening a file outside a project
@section "Timed out after 30 seconds" opening a file outside a project

A file in a Lake package is served by the Lean its "lean-toolchain" pins.
A file outside one is served by whatever elan's @emph{default} toolchain
resolves to -- normally the @samp{stable} channel, which moves with every Lean
release.  The first time it names a version this machine has not got, elan
downloads the whole toolchain before the server can start, and Eglot gives
up after @code{eglot-connect-timeout} seconds, which is 30 by default.

The download is what is slow, not this package, and it only happens once
per version.  Either warm it first:

@example
elan toolchain install stable
@end example


-- or @samp{M-x lean4-install-toolchain} -- or give the connection longer:

@lisp
(setq eglot-connect-timeout 300)
@end lisp

@samp{M-x lean4-diagnose} reports which toolchain a buffer resolved to, which
is the quickest way to tell a pinned version from the default.

@node "Imports are out of date and must be rebuilt"
@section "Imports are out of date and must be rebuilt"

Lean says this, and adds @emph{use the "Restart File" command in your editor}.
That command is @samp{C-c C-d}, which is @code{lean4-refresh-file-dependencies} and
also @code{lean4-restart-file} under the name Lean uses; it is @samp{Restart File}
in the @samp{Lean} menu.  VS Code registers both names for it too.

Visiting a file does not build what it imports -- see @ref{Building dependencies} -- so this is what Lean says when an import has been
rebuilt, or edited, since the file was last opened.  On a project that
depends on Mathlib, fetch the cache before rebuilding, or @samp{C-c C-d} will
compile it locally:

@example
C-c C-p C-c   then   C-c C-d
@end example


Lean words it two ways, and they mean different things.  @emph{Must} be
rebuilt is an error in the file's header: nothing in the file elaborates
until it is dealt with.  @emph{Should} be rebuilt arrives while the file is
open, when a dependency has gone stale underneath it; the file still
works, it is just no longer current.

The second comes at information severity, which
@code{lean4-flymake-minimum-severity} keeps out of the editor by default, so
look for it under @samp{All messages} in the goal display rather than as an
underline.  Set that option to @samp{note} to have it flagged in the buffer
too.

@node Common Pitfalls
@chapter Common Pitfalls

Lean4-Mode only supports version 4 of Lean.  For editing Lean version
3, use @uref{https://github.com/leanprover/lean3-mode, Lean3-Mode}, which is also known as Lean-Mode due to historical
reasons.  In principle, it is fine to have both Lean3-Mode and
Lean4-Mode installed at the same time.  But note that Lean3-Mode uses
the prefix @samp{lean-} for its symbols.  E.g. you should not use
@samp{lean-}-prefixed commands in a buffer with Lean4-Mode as major mode.

